{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-48496",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}((c-x)^{2}+3c) \\\\  & =(c-(-1))^{2}+3c \\\\  & ={c}^{2}+2c+1+3c \\\\  & ={c}^{2}+5c+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {c}^{2}+5c+1 & =-5 \\\\ {c}^{2}+5c+6 & =0 \\\\ (c+2)(c+3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $c=-2$ or $c=-3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
